{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat VENUE.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,135p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat .agentic_paper_workspace.json; ls source_evidence; cat paper_prep_20261117_131854.json; head -70 paper.sty; cat reference_query_plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n{\n  \"global_memory_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"output_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/paper\",\n  \"results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"task_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/task\"\n}\ndownstream\n{\n  \"effective_results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"mode\": \"direct\",\n  \"paper_results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"requested_results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"source_results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\"\n}%%%% ICLR Macros (LaTex)\n%%%% Adapted by Hugo Larochelle from the NIPS stylefile Macros\n%%%% Style File\n%%%% Dec 12, 1990   Rev Aug 14, 1991; Sept, 1995; April, 1997; April, 1999; October 2014\n\n% This file can be used with Latex2e whether running in main mode, or\n% 2.09 compatibility mode.\n%\n% If using main mode, you need to include the commands\n%             \\documentclass{article}\n%             \\usepackage{iclr14submit_e,times}\n%\n\n% Change the overall width of the page.  If these parameters are\n%       changed, they will require corresponding changes in the\n%       maketitle section.\n%\n\\usepackage{eso-pic} % used by \\AddToShipoutPicture\n\\RequirePackage{fancyhdr}\n\\RequirePackage{natbib}\n\n% modification to natbib citations\n\\setcitestyle{authoryear,round,citesep={;},aysep={,},yysep={;}}\n\n\\renewcommand{\\topfraction}{0.95}   % let figure take up nearly whole page\n\\renewcommand{\\textfraction}{0.05}  % let figure take up nearly whole page\n\n% Define iclrfinal, set to true if iclrfinalcopy is defined\n\\newif\\ificlrfinal\n\\iclrfinalfalse\n\\def\\iclrfinalcopy{\\iclrfinaltrue}\n\\font\\iclrtenhv  = phvb at 8pt\n\n% Specify the dimensions of each page\n\n\\setlength{\\paperheight}{11in}\n\\setlength{\\paperwidth}{8.5in}\n\n\n\\oddsidemargin .5in    %   Note \\oddsidemargin = \\evensidemargin\n\\evensidemargin .5in\n\\marginparwidth 0.07 true in\n%\\marginparwidth 0.75 true in\n%\\topmargin 0 true pt           % Nominal distance from top of page to top of\n%\\topmargin 0.125in\n\\topmargin -0.625in\n\\addtolength{\\headsep}{0.25in}\n\\textheight 9.0 true in       % Height of text (including footnotes & figures)\n\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n\\widowpenalty=10000\n\\clubpenalty=10000\n\n% \\thispagestyle{empty}        \\pagestyle{empty}\n\\flushbottom \\sloppy\n\n% We're never going to need a table of contents, so just flush it to\n% save space --- suggested by drstrip@sandia-2\n\\def\\addcontentsline#1#2#3{}\n\n% Title stuff, taken from deproc.\n\\def\\maketitle{\\par\n\\begingroup\n   \\def\\thefootnote{\\fnsymbol{footnote}}\n   \\def\\@makefnmark{\\hbox to 0pt{$^{\\@thefnmark}$\\hss}} % for perfect author\n                                                        % name centering\n%   The footnote-mark was overlapping the footnote-text,\n%   added the following to fix this problem               (MK)\n   \\long\\def\\@makefntext##1{\\parindent 1em\\noindent\n                            \\hbox to1.8em{\\hss $\\m@th ^{\\@thefnmark}$}##1}\n   \\@maketitle \\@thanks\n# Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status: ready.** Nine queries; planning only. No literature was retrieved, verified, or read in this stage. Titles and identifiers below come from the permitted task starters, not a new search. Existing `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` are absent, so there is no prior query provenance to reuse or correct.\n\n## Scientific Focus\n\nThe paper concerns conditional task-support investment needed for a fixed target model to become strictly first on public multi-task score matrices under mean, median, and trimmed-mean aggregation. Investment is the number or fraction of selected task coordinates. Selected scores follow `x_j + alpha*(1-x_j)` with `alpha` equal to 0.25, 0.5, or 1; rival scores remain fixed. This is a score-space counterfactual, not an observed training response, monetary cost, or causal estimate of contamination.\n\n## Grounding and Evidence Limits\n\n- `paper_data.json`, fields `downstream_study` and `final_evaluation`: the search found no qualifying winner, selected the baseline, and completed a qualified final evaluation. The final aggregate feasible rate is 0.6072530864197531 and mean verified gap fraction is 0.046025557721464154. These motivate attainability and certificate-gap themes, not an ordering of aggregation rules. Search and final scores have different evaluation scopes and are not evidence of improvement over one another.\n- `source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py`: selected-method evidence is exact sorted-headroom selection for the mean, exhaustive support enumeration on at most six coordinates, and deterministic marginal-aggregate greedy selection on larger median/trimmed panels. The conventional even median averages two middle scores; trimming removes `floor(0.2*m)` values at each end. Strict first requires exceeding all rivals by more than `1e-10`.\n- `global_memory/task/evaluate.py` and the final solution structure in `paper_data.json`: actual comparison controls are no intervention, random-order support selection, and marginal greedy. The workload includes ordinary, constant-score, zero-uplift copy, and joint-permutation cases. These integrity checks do not constitute an architecture comparison. Candidate-supplied diagnostics are explicitly marked unverified; they are not independent scientific evidence.\n- `task/resources/final/manifest.json` and `task/resources/final/records.json`: locally recorded acquisition time is `2026-11-17T11:35:39.193972+00:00`; the records SHA-256 is `d80abb1b13c6962e15f04d9794fc18fcf39e91cf7c79c679e233d3031f934398`. The manifest retains individual source hashes and limitations. These are inspected local provenance, not a fresh network verification.\n- `figures/` is empty and `paper_data.json` contains no ablation studies. No figures, matched ablations, universal robustness order, or successful rank-window/clipped-bound improvement can ground additional queries.\n\n## Methods and Expected Comparators\n\nThe central methodological neighborhood is finite-sample sensitivity of means and order-statistic aggregators to sparse, bounded, one-sided coordinate changes. Distinguish exact minimum support from a feasible witness, a conservative lower bound, and proven infeasibility. Classical replacement breakdown is related context, not an interchangeable definition of the run's investment metric. The elementary mean sorting argument and exhaustive audit do not warrant a generic optimization citation search.\n\nReviewer-facing comparators are social-choice evaluation, strategic post-training incentives, benchmark task-selection sensitivity, and evaluation-data contamination. Social-choice or equilibrium models are related work, not executed baselines. Borda and multi-developer equilibria were not tested. The selected run does not support proposed coupled-clipped or rank-window methods as improvements.\n\n## Benchmark Scope and Architecture\n\n- **HELM/MMLU:** release `v1.13.0`, with 57 subject coordinates. Cite MMLU for task construction and HELM for the evaluation framework; neither paper replaces snapshot provenance. The manifest warns that evaluation conditions need not be identical across model rows.\n- **Open LLM contents:** revision `9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2`; six raw-score coordinates are IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MUSR, and MMLU-PRO. Dataset citation metadata should come directly from the pinned official record; do not invent a paper or data license.\n- **BBH sensitivity:** results revision `aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5`, with complete 24-subtask panels and exact model-revision matching. The manifest says this sensitivity is separately reported and not scored or merged into the primary six-task contents scores. Contents and results are distinct runs; revision matching does not prove equivalent evaluation settings.\n- **Architectural shifts:** none supported; the JSON field is empty. Subject-level versus task-level panels are a granularity distinction, not a representation or architecture transition. BBH's source-paper title does not imply a chain-of-thought experiment here.\n\n## Query Set\n\n1. **Core method:** `median trimmed mean finite-sample replacement breakdown order statistics` — foundational context for the actual robust aggregators and sparse-coordinate sensitivity; screen independently for foundational statistics sources, not leaderboard-specific discoveries.\n2. **Comparator:** `\"Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\" 2312.03121` — cardinal aggregation versus social-choice evaluation; no suggestion that ordinal aggregation was implemented.\n3. **Comparator:** `\"Leaderboard Incentives: Model Rankings under Strategic Post-Training\" 2603.08371` — compare incentive-model assumptions with a single target, fixed rivals, and externally assumed response ceilings. Read before asserting details.\n4. **Result-driven theme:** `\"The Benchmark Lottery\" 2107.07002` — task-panel sensitivity and the distinction between panel selection and aggregation; no presumed rule-order reversal.\n5. **Benchmark:** `\"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\" 2009.03300` — the 57-subject MMLU structure, not the later HELM snapshot settings.\n6. **Benchmark:** `\"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\" 2211.09110` — the framework supplying HELM results, paired with the pinned local provenance.\n7. **Benchmark:** `\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\" 2210.09261` — identity of the separate BBH sensitivity panel, not a prompting or architecture comparison.\n8. **Benchmark:** `\"open-llm-leaderboard/contents\" \"9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2\"` — citation metadata for the actual six-task snapshot; resolve directly at the official dataset revision if indexing is insufficient. Preserve the separate results revision for BBH provenance.\n9. **Comparator:** `\"Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\" 2311.01964` — contamination motivation and the boundary between assumed score improvements and measured training effects.\n\n## Result-Driven Priorities\n\nPrioritize response-ceiling-dependent attainability, support cardinality, and exact versus bounded certificates. Treat panel granularity as a scope variable rather than a demonstrated transfer effect. The selected baseline is the supported configuration; no search candidate has an established improvement. Do not search as though one aggregation rule is universally most robust or actual training costs were measured.\n\n## Exclusions and Retrieval Safety\n\nThe prohibited prior study is intentionally unidentified. Do not infer its title, authors, or results. The later retrieval stage must prefer direct canonical records for the explicitly permitted starter identifiers and pinned dataset metadata. Do not expand citation graphs, references, cited-by lists, related-paper recommendations, surveys, discussion pages, mirrors, or repositories. Exact-title lookup is not permission to read arbitrary surrounding pages. If source isolation cannot be ensured, skip the unsafe query rather than broaden it.\n\nThe only topical query without a supplied identifier is foundational robust statistics; do not combine it with leaderboard manipulation or targeted-investment terms. If prohibited material is accidentally encountered, stop reading immediately and record the location and amount exposed in the reproducibility record. No such external encounter occurred during this local-only stage.\n\nAvoid broad searches paraphrasing the research question; fabricated established names for local proposals; unsupported Borda, equilibrium, training, architecture, and chain-of-thought claims; presumed universal robustness or rule reversals; unpinned latest leaderboards; Arena-specific detours; and standalone searches for every component benchmark without an actual comparison. Generic optimization, generic benchmarking, scientific-method, writing-process, citation-volume, and campaign-chronology searches would dilute the actual score-intervention and order-statistic story. Exact permitted identifiers and the pinned dataset record provide more precise, safer coverage. The lm-evaluation-harness starter need not become a separate query merely because public score files were produced by an evaluation stack; this run does not evaluate models anew.\n\n## Handoff\n\nThe JSON mirrors this memo with nine nonduplicate queries and no architecture query. Planning readiness means the literature neighborhood is grounded, not that references or scientific claims have been verified. Future retrieval must establish existence, metadata, substantive relevance, and actual reading before citation. This stage does not write bibliography, provenance, LaTeX, or claims-grounding artifacts and does not run any retrieval service.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
